# NetworkManager -- the daemon gnome-control-center's Network and Wi-Fi panels
# are a front end to.
#
# It contains NO 802.11 authentication code and NO netlink library. It drives
# wpa_supplicant over D-Bus for Wi-Fi (see pkgs/wpa_supplicant/pkg.env) and
# talks to the kernel through its own netlink layer rather than libnl (see
# pkgs/libnl/pkg.env, which exists for wpa_supplicant, not for this).
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env -- versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh.
# NOT ON download.gnome.org, AND THE OBVIOUS URL WOULD HAVE BEEN WRONG IN A WAY
# THAT HIDES. /sources/NetworkManager/ exists and dead-ends at 1.51 -- releases
# moved to freedesktop's GitLab -- so a URL written from the GNOME convention
# 404s. It would also have passed every local check, because a tarball already
# in ~/.cache/duct/sources is never re-fetched; only a cold CI fetch would have
# found it. This is finding 12 one module further on: the book's URL is the
# authority, not the pattern the neighbouring recipes use.
#
# Verified rather than assumed: fetched from the book's URL and compared. The
# bytes are identical to the cached tarball, and the md5 matches the one BLFS
# 12.4 publishes on its NetworkManager page (300509d31ac641bba24240d708916083).
SRC_URL=https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/NetworkManager/NetworkManager/-/releases/1.54.0/downloads/NetworkManager-1.54.0.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=6c104c8e0bd5a3bbbb9ce28b7670e72924e058fa9303e90dab2815d70a530879
SRC_DIR=NetworkManager-1.54.0

# EVERY FLAG BELOW WAS DECIDED AGAINST meson.build AND meson_options.txt IN THE
# TARBALL, with line numbers, because this options file is the most misleading
# one in the tree: it has 67 options, a third of them default to a thing this
# distribution does not have, and three of them are hard requirements dressed as
# optional dependencies.
#
# -Dpolkit=true. THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS FILE AND IT LOOKS LIKE A
# DEFAULT. It is the default, and it is written down anyway, because
# -Dpolkit=false does not merely drop a dependency: meson.build 518-522 reads
#
#     config_auth_polkit_default = get_option('config_auth_polkit_default')
#     if config_auth_polkit_default == 'default'
#       config_auth_polkit_default = (enable_polkit ? 'true' : 'false')
#     config_h.set_quoted('NM_CONFIG_DEFAULT_MAIN_AUTH_POLKIT', ...)
#
# so turning polkit off compiles in main.auth-polkit=false, and NetworkManager
# then performs NO AUTHORIZATION AT ALL on its D-Bus API -- any local user may
# reconfigure the network, add a connection, or read every stored secret. That
# is a security posture chosen by a build flag that reads like a size
# optimisation. polkit 126 is packaged, so there is no reason to be near it.
#
# TWO THAT LOOK OPTIONAL AND ARE NOT. Each is finding 1's shape -- a
# dependency(..., required: false) followed immediately by an assert on
# .found() -- so meson_options.txt showing a boolean tells you nothing:
#   -Dnmtui=false          meson.build 809-810, libnewt. Not packaged.
#   -Dnbft=false           meson.build 938-939, libnvme. Not packaged.
#
# -Dmodem_manager=true, AND THE ORDERING THAT MADE IT POSSIBLE. meson.build
# 610-611 is a BARE dependency('mm-glib') -- not even required:false, simply
# unconditional whenever the option is on -- so this flag and the package it
# needs had to be sequenced rather than assumed. The agreed order was
# libgudev -> ModemManager -> NetworkManager, and it held: libgudev published,
# ModemManager was built and merged against it, and this flag was turned on
# while THIS package was still banked and unpublished.
#
# That timing is the whole point. -Dmodem_manager is not a flag that can be
# flipped later for free: it changes [dependencies], which reaches
# TAPEPACKAGE.toml, so doing it after publication would cost a subversion bump
# and a full rebuild. Turning it on now costs an edit. WITHOUT IT THERE IS NO
# MOBILE BROADBAND PANEL AT ALL.
#
# What that panel can actually drive is a separate and narrower question, and
# it is recorded in pkgs/ModemManager/pkg.env rather than repeated here:
# ModemManager is built without MBIM, QMI and QRTR, so the AT-command path is
# what remains. Deferred deliberately, not overlooked.
#
# -Dsystemdsystemunitdir=no. NOT COSMETIC, AND NOT SKIPPABLE. The option is a
# STRING defaulting to '', and meson.build 377-382 treats '' as "go and find
# it": `assert(systemd_dep.found(), 'systemd required but not found...')`.
# elogind provides libelogind.pc and a libsystemd.pc symlink, but NOT the
# systemd.pc this looks for, so leaving the default fails the build. An empty
# string meaning "yes, auto-detect" rather than "no" is the same trap as an
# `auto` feature, wearing different clothes.
#
# -Dsession_tracking=elogind and -Dsuspend_resume=elogind. session_tracking
# defaults to 'systemd' outright; suspend_resume defaults to 'auto', which
# prefers systemd and falls back to elogind. meson.build 417-419 asserts
# libelogind >= 229 is present -- ours is 255.17, and pkgs/elogind's
# post-install.sh is what guarantees libelogind.pc exists.
# -Dsession_tracking_consolekit=false because there is no ConsoleKit either,
# and it defaults true.
#
# -Dselinux=false, -Dsystemd_journal=false, -Dlibaudit=no. All three default
# ON, and none of libselinux, the journal or libaudit exists here. Left to
# themselves they are probe-and-degrade rather than hard errors, which is worse:
# the build would succeed and quietly ship a NetworkManager with no audit
# logging on a system where that had never been decided.
#
# -Dcrypto=gnutls. THE 802.1X CERTIFICATE PATH, and the history matters because
# the obvious setting would have shipped a quiet hole.
#
# The choices are nss, gnutls and null. meson.build 542-560 probes both nss and
# gnutls with required:false and then asserts on the one selected -- finding 1's
# shape again, so the option combo tells you nothing about what is required.
# Neither backend was packaged when this recipe was written, which left only
# 'null', and null is not a lightweight fallback:
#
#   src/libnm-crypto/nm-crypto-null.c returns FALSE from EVERY entry point,
#   including _nm_crypto_init. Its caller is
#   src/libnm-core-impl/nm-setting-8021x.c line 533,
#   nm_crypto_load_and_verify_certificate, which runs when a certificate is SET
#   on a connection. So with null, WPA-Enterprise with a CA or client
#   certificate cannot be configured at all -- the panel refuses the file --
#   while username/password PEAP keeps working, which is exactly why it would
#   have looked fine.
#
# gnutls was then brought into scope and assigned elsewhere, so this recipe
# waits for it rather than shipping the hole. That was free to change only
# because this package is banked and has never been published: [dependencies]
# reaches TAPEPACKAGE.toml, so adding one after publication costs a subversion
# bump and a rebuild.
#
# post-install.sh asserts the OUTCOME rather than the flag: nm-crypto-gnutls.c
# is compiled into a static library and linked into libnm, so the proof is a
# DT_NEEDED on libgnutls -- which -Dcrypto=null would not produce and no
# configuration summary can fake.
#
# -Dreadline=auto WITH -Dnmcli=true, and the two are connected by an assert
# most readers will not expect. meson.build 803:
#   assert(with_readline != 'none', 'nmcli requires readline library')
# and 777-800 resolve `auto` by probing libreadline, then libedit, then setting
# 'none' -- so with neither packaged, `auto` does not degrade, IT FAILS THE
# BUILD. This recipe therefore cannot be built until readline is published, and
# that is deliberate: -Dnmcli=false would build today and produce a
# NetworkManager with no command-line interface at all, on a system whose GUI
# does not exist yet. A network stack nobody can drive is worse than a wait.
#
# -Dovs=true is what jansson is for; -Dconcheck=true is what curl is for
# (connectivity checking, which is how the panel knows it has internet rather
# than merely an address); -Dlibpsl=true for cookie domain rules.
#
# -Dudev_dir and -Ddbus_conf_dir are both passed although both defaults already
# resolve correctly (meson.build 366-374 and 562-565). Named for the reason
# fuse3 and bluez name theirs: the defaults are derived from pkg-config, and a
# derivation that moves would move these paths with it. /lib is a symlink
# duct-filesystem owns.
#
# -Dtests=no, -Ddocs=false, -Dfirewalld_zone=false (no firewalld),
# -Dnm_cloud_setup=false (cloud metadata polling on a desktop image),
# -Dqt=false and -Dvapi=false (both `auto`, and both would switch themselves on
# in a tree that shipped Qt or vala -- pinned so they stay decisions),
# -Dppp=false (pppd not packaged), -Diwd=false (we ship wpa_supplicant).
#
# -Dconfig_wifi_backend_default=wpa_supplicant states in the shipped
# configuration what the rest of this chain assumes. -Dconfig_dhcp_default=internal
# needs no external client. -Dconfig_logging_backend_default=syslog because the
# journal is off and 'default' would otherwise be decided elsewhere.
MESON_ARGS="-Dpolkit=true \
-Dsession_tracking=elogind -Dsuspend_resume=elogind -Dsession_tracking_consolekit=false \
-Dsystemdsystemunitdir=no -Dudev_dir=/usr/lib/udev \
-Ddbus_conf_dir=/usr/share/dbus-1/system.d \
-Dselinux=false -Dsystemd_journal=false -Dlibaudit=no \
-Dnmtui=false -Dnbft=false -Dmodem_manager=true \
-Dppp=false -Dqt=false -Dvapi=false -Diwd=false \
-Dovs=true -Dconcheck=true -Dlibpsl=true -Dcrypto=gnutls \
-Dnmcli=true -Dreadline=auto -Dintrospection=true \
-Dconfig_wifi_backend_default=wpa_supplicant \
-Dconfig_logging_backend_default=syslog \
-Dconfig_dhcp_default=internal \
-Dtests=no -Ddocs=false -Dfirewalld_zone=false -Dnm_cloud_setup=false"
